<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-018> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) T1917-018 — Plateau tri-junction (120 degree) claim: Lami balance, periodic froth relaxation, junction-angle statistics (species-agnostic claim; specimens: Homo sapiens corneal endothelium, Allium cepa epidermis, Thompson Fig. 103 (Zea mays parenchyma), Fig. 124 (Girardia epidermis))",
        "Thompson (1917) T1917-018 — Plateau tri-junction (120 degree) claim: Lami balance, periodic froth relaxation, junction-angle statistics faithfully reproduces species-agnostic claim; specimens: Homo sapiens corneal endothelium, Allium cepa epidermis, Thompson Fig. 103 (Zea mays parenchyma), Fig. 124 (Girardia epidermis) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-5>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/thompson1917-t1917-018-6> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) thompson/claims/t1917-018_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real species-agnostic claim; specimens: Homo sapiens corneal endothelium, Allium cepa epidermis, Thompson Fig. 103 (Zea mays parenchyma), Fig. 124 (Girardia epidermis) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-018_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py> ;
    schema:about "species-agnostic claim; specimens: Homo sapiens corneal endothelium, Allium cepa epidermis, Thompson Fig. 103 (Zea mays parenchyma), Fig. 124 (Girardia epidermis)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Does not claim that junction angles are exactly 120 degrees in any tissue, that cortical tensions are measured (they are inferred by inverse Lami only), or anything about the pre-scouted Dryad set doi:10.5061/dryad.mn8cs, which is a DATASET_MISMATCH (Ca2+ ratio imaging with no junction label) and is not used.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not model cell division, active junction remodelling, or 3D apico-basal geometry.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Lami/Neumann triangle of forces (E1) with the Fig. 113 cases and the inverse map from measured angles to tension ratios; Steiner-vs-cross film lengths (E2); angle-sum identity statistics with vertex-level bootstrap (E3); periodic Poisson-Voronoi vertex network relaxed by analytic-gradient descent with T1 exchanges under E = sum K/2 (A - A0)^2 + Gamma/2 P^2 + sum Lambda(phi) l, Lambda(phi) = Lambda0 (1 + eps cos 2 phi) (E5), in the equal-tension Plateau limit (Gamma = eps = 0), the Farhadifar Case I comparator, and a 7-point eps sweep.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Segmentation of two CC BY-SA micrographs (nucleus-seeded watershed on a Sato ridge image for the corneal endothelium; Sato wall mask -> connected components for the onion epidermis) and skeleton-graph extraction of Thompson's Figs. 103 and 124; tri-cellular junction angles by a chord estimator (straight edges to the neighbouring vertices, primary) and a branch estimator (mean wall offset within R = L_e/3).",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Verdict by pre-registered criteria (three-fold fraction >= 0.90 in both micrographs; chord median |theta - 120| <= 15 degrees in the isotropic specimen; dispersion below the Poisson-Voronoi null): SUPPORTED, with the branch-estimator sensitivity (15.9 degrees) reported.",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson (1917) p. 297: three similar liquid surfaces always meet at 120 degrees; p. 303 Fig. 113 cases (1)-(3): 90 / >90 / 120 degrees as the partition tension t rises to T; pp. 309-310: Lamarle's least-area partition meets in threes at 120 degrees and 'in any ordinary section of a vegetable parenchyma ... cell-walls everywhere meeting, by threes, at angles of 120 degrees, irrespective of the size of the individual cells'.",
        "reported_in_source: Thompson (1917) pp. 314, 321-322: epidermal outer walls and growth/traction anisotropy (Fig. 124 Girardia) are stated exceptions where the angles depart from 120 degrees while three-fold meeting persists.",
        "transfer_boundary: 2D en-face projection / plane section of 3D cells; Thompson's 109 deg 28' tetrahedral edge law is not simulated. The vertex model has straight edges, so unlike a real soap froth it cannot realise 120 degrees at every vertex of a disordered tessellation (a straight-sided pentagon's angles sum to 540 degrees); its irreducible chord-angle SD (~10 degrees) is the appropriate null for straight-walled tissue, not an exact 120.",
        "transfer_boundary: The Commons micrographs are modern specimens not figured by Thompson; because the claim names no species they are specimens of the claim rather than analogues of a named species, and are labelled as modern throughout." ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-018_the-forms-of-tissues-or-cell-aggregates.py; output: docs/media/t1917_018_the_forms_of_tissues_or_cell_a_comparison.png; organism: species-agnostic claim; specimens: Homo sapiens corneal endothelium, Allium cepa epidermis, Thompson Fig. 103 (Zea mays parenchyma), Fig. 124 (Girardia epidermis); environment: python 3.12.3, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, Pillow 13.0.0, scikit-image 0.26.0; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Thompson (1917) p. 297: three similar liquid surfaces always meet at 120 degrees; p. 303 Fig. 113 cases (1)-(3): 90 / >90 / 120 degrees as the partition tension t rises to T; pp. 309-310: Lamarle's least-area partition meets in threes at 120 degrees and 'in any ordinary section of a vegetable parenchyma ... cell-walls everywhere meeting, by threes, at angles of 120 degrees, irrespective of the size of the individual cells'. | Thompson (1917) pp. 314, 321-322: epidermal outer walls and growth/traction anisotropy (Fig. 124 Girardia) are stated exceptions where the angles depart from 120 degrees while three-fold meeting persists.; reconstructed_in_script: Lami/Neumann triangle of forces (E1) with the Fig. 113 cases and the inverse map from measured angles to tension ratios; Steiner-vs-cross film lengths (E2); angle-sum identity statistics with vertex-level bootstrap (E3); periodic Poisson-Voronoi vertex network relaxed by analytic-gradient descent with T1 exchanges under E = sum K/2 (A - A0)^2 + Gamma/2 P^2 + sum Lambda(phi) l, Lambda(phi) = Lambda0 (1 + eps cos 2 phi) (E5), in the equal-tension Plateau limit (Gamma = eps = 0), the Farhadifar Case I comparator, and a 7-point eps sweep. | Segmentation of two CC BY-SA micrographs (nucleus-seeded watershed on a Sato ridge image for the corneal endothelium; Sato wall mask -> connected components for the onion epidermis) and skeleton-graph extraction of Thompson's Figs. 103 and 124; tri-cellular junction angles by a chord estimator (straight edges to the neighbouring vertices, primary) and a branch estimator (mean wall offset within R = L_e/3). | Verdict by pre-registered criteria (three-fold fraction >= 0.90 in both micrographs; chord median |theta - 120| <= 15 degrees in the isotropic specimen; dispersion below the Poisson-Voronoi null): SUPPORTED, with the branch-estimator sensitivity (15.9 degrees) reported.; transfer_boundary: 2D en-face projection / plane section of 3D cells; Thompson's 109 deg 28' tetrahedral edge law is not simulated. The vertex model has straight edges, so unlike a real soap froth it cannot realise 120 degrees at every vertex of a disordered tessellation (a straight-sided pentagon's angles sum to 540 degrees); its irreducible chord-angle SD (~10 degrees) is the appropriate null for straight-walled tissue, not an exact 120. | The Commons micrographs are modern specimens not figured by Thompson; because the claim names no species they are specimens of the claim rather than analogues of a named species, and are labelled as modern throughout.; does_not_claim: Does not claim that junction angles are exactly 120 degrees in any tissue, that cortical tensions are measured (they are inferred by inverse Lami only), or anything about the pre-scouted Dryad set doi:10.5061/dryad.mn8cs, which is a DATASET_MISMATCH (Ca2+ ratio imaging with no junction label) and is not used. | Does not model cell division, active junction remodelling, or 3D apico-basal geometry.]; other sources: On Growth and Form (Ch. VII, The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates, pp. 293-345, Figs. 100-135) <https://archive.org/details/ongrowthform1917thom>, Statique experimentale et theorique des liquides soumis aux seules forces moleculaires <https://archive.org/details/statiqueexprim01plat>, Morphology of human corneal endothelium in situ (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, SHA-256 9daed5fb...) <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morphology_of_human_corneal_endothelium_in_situ.jpg>, Onion epidermis (microscopic image) 007533276M (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, original SHA-256 6430f011...) <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onion_epidermis_(microscopic_image)_007533276M.jpg>; phenomenon_size: 979 µm (0.0009792 m, log10=-3.009 m, tier=5_primordium_100um); species_scale_ladder (20 runs from 979 µm to 2.85 m): 979 µm:cortical-sulcal-axon-tension, 1.4 mm:bressloff-cowan-V1-hallucination, 1.5 mm:corticothalamic-seizure-spirals, 1.7 mm:homo-sapiens-bioelectric-polarity, 3.1 mm:vertex-glass-jamming, 3.4 mm:homo-sapiens-vertex-packing, 6.3 cm:homo-sapiens-turing-wavelength, 11.7 cm:homo-sapiens-clock-wavefront" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-9235.1989.tb02334.x>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2007.11.049>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02952>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2015.0520>,
        <https://doi.org/10.2307/1970949> ;
